const enBody = `<h1 id="top">Brave Search API Review 2026: The Independent Index After Bing</h1>

<p><strong>November 14, 2026</strong> — Two of the three ways to buy a first-party web-search API on the public internet are now closed. Microsoft retired the Bing Search APIs on <strong>August 11, 2025</strong>, and Google closed the Custom Search JSON API to new customers with a <strong>January 1, 2027</strong> discontinuation date. What is left, for anyone building an agent that needs to read the live web, is a market of aggregators, scrapers, and one vendor that still runs its own crawler and its own index.</p>

<p>Brave Search API is that vendor. It bills from <strong>$5 per 1,000 requests</strong> with no subscription, it serves every query from its own index with no pass-through to Google or Bing, and it publishes a separate <strong>Answers</strong> tier at $4 per 1,000 requests plus $5 per million input and output tokens. This review covers what each plan actually buys, where the cost model breaks for agent workloads, and the two contract terms that will decide whether you are allowed to build the thing you want to build.</p>

<h2 id="price-card">The price card, plan by plan</h2>

<p>Brave sells three products on one page, priced on completely different axes. The table is the full public pricing, transcribed from Brave's own API page. The first plan bills by the request. The second bills by the request <em>and</em> by the token. The third is a contract.</p>

<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Plan</th><th>Price</th><th>Rate limit</th><th>What you get</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Search</strong></td><td>$5 / 1,000 requests</td><td>50 queries/second</td><td>URLs, text, news, images; Goggles reranking; extra alternate snippets; schema-enriched results with added metadata</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Answers</strong></td><td>$4 / 1,000 requests + $5 per million input/output tokens</td><td>2 queries/second</td><td>Summarized answers grounded on one or multiple searches, with citations, streaming, and OpenAI SDK compatibility</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Enterprise</strong></td><td>Custom terms</td><td>Custom</td><td>Bespoke capacity, custom endpoints, NDAs, invoicing, enterprise support, full-funnel Zero Data Retention</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Two things in that table decide most deployments. First, <strong>every paid plan includes $5 in free credits every month, automatically applied</strong> — one thousand Search queries before you pay anything — and a credit card is required at signup purely as an anti-fraud check, never charged for free usage. Second, Brave sells <strong>prepaid credits rather than a subscription</strong>: you load a balance and draw it down, no monthly fee, no lock-in. There is also a non-card path: Brave accepts <strong>x402 micropayments in USDC on Base</strong>, where a signed payment acts as the credential in place of an API key.</p>

<p>The math works out to $0.005 per Search query. A pipeline making 100,000 queries a month pays $500; an agent loop making 10,000 calls a day pays about $1,500 — and at that volume you are probably already in Enterprise territory, because the 50 queries/second ceiling is the real limit, not the price.</p>

<p>For comparison, Google's own Custom Search JSON API pricing, before it disappears, is <strong>$5 per 1,000 queries on top of 100 free queries per day</strong>, capped at 10,000 queries per day. The per-thousand price is identical to Brave's. The horizon is not: Google's has a date on it, Brave's does not.</p>

<h2 id="why-now">Why the two exits changed the comparison</h2>

<p>Until now, Brave's pitch was a privacy argument, and privacy arguments lose to habit. The habits were <code>googleapis.com/customsearch/v1</code> and <code>api.bing.microsoft.com</code>: three lines of code.</p>

<p>Microsoft's own developer documentation is unambiguous: "Public Bing Search and Bing Custom Search APIs were retired on 11th August 2025." The Bing Web Search overview page now carries <code>is_archived</code> and <code>is_retired</code> markers and lives under a <code>/previous-versions/</code> breadcrumb. The pricing page that carried the per-thousand rates is gone, serving a Microsoft "page you requested cannot be found" notice.</p>

<p>Google's documentation is equally unambiguous, and importantly it distinguishes the two audiences: "This API is not available for new customers." The pricing "applies only to existing Custom Search JSON API customers until the service discontinuation on January 1, 2027." New customers are already past. Existing ones get a date.</p>

<p>That changes the decision from "should I switch for privacy" to "what do I switch <em>to</em>." Brave is not the only independent option — you could build a scraper on top of Serper, or route through a provider that fronts a US/EU search engine. But Brave is the one publishing a per-request price, an independent index, and a token-priced answer endpoint on the same page, with a token-balanced context endpoint built for agent grounding.</p>

<p>It is worth being precise about what Brave's index is. It is <strong>not</strong> a repackaging of Bing or Google results: Brave runs its own crawler, its own ranking models, and its own index, and states there is "no fallback to another search engine for any query." Its framing goes further, and that is the part worth arguing with: "Google and Bing have shut down their services; needle tests prove all other services scrape."</p>

<h2 id="llm-context">The LLM Context endpoint is the actual product</h2>

<p>This matters if you are building an agent rather than a search box. Brave's argument: a results page gives you titles and snippets, while an agent needs parsed, pre-formatted, pre-filtered material. The <code>/res/v1/llm/context</code> endpoint returns ranked passages from the best pages in a single call — and it handles content types that plain web search does not: text, tables, code, JSON, forum threads, and video captions.</p>

<pre><code is:raw>curl -s --compressed "https://api.search.brave.com/res/v1/llm/context?q=EU+AI+Act+enforcement+timeline" \
  -H "Accept: application/json" \
  -H "X-Subscription-Token: &lt;YOUR_API_KEY&gt;" \
  --data-urlencode "maximum_number_of_tokens=4096" \
  --data-urlencode "context_threshold_mode=strict"
</code></pre>

<pre><code is:raw>import requests

r = requests.post(
    "https://api.search.brave.com/res/v1/llm/context",
    headers={
        "accept": "application/json",
        "X-Subscription-Token": "YOUR_API_KEY",
    },
    json={"q": "what changed in EU AI Act enforcement this quarter"},
    timeout=30,
)
r.raise_for_status()
payload = r.json()

for src in payload.get("sources", []):
    print(src["url"], src.get("fetched_content_timestamp"))
</code></pre>

<p>The token budget is the parameter that matters. You set <code>maximum_number_of_tokens</code> (1,024 to 32,768, default 8,192) and optionally cap <code>maximum_number_of_tokens_per_url</code> (512 to 8,192, default 4,096). Brave's guidance is to leave <code>count</code> and the per-URL cap unset and tune only the total budget: 2,048 for a simple factual question, 8,192 for a standard query, 16,384 for complex research. Larger budgets mean more information and more inference cost.</p>

<p>Relevance filtering is a separate lever: <code>context_threshold_mode</code> takes <code>strict</code> (fewer but more relevant results), <code>balanced</code>, <code>lenient</code>, and <code>disabled</code>. Leave it unset and Brave applies its own calibrated default. There is also <code>enable_source_metadata</code>, which adds <code>site_name</code>, <code>favicon</code>, <code>description</code>, and a <code>fetched_content_timestamp</code> to every source entry — the timestamp is what lets a downstream model tell a fact from a two-year-old forum post that happens to match the query.</p>

<p>Three details are worth internalizing. The endpoint is <code>/res/v1/llm/context</code>, not <code>/res/v1/web/search</code> — different products with different shapes. Authentication uses <code>X-Subscription-Token</code>, not <code>Authorization: Bearer</code>, so a generic OpenAI-compatible client needs a header shim. And Brave's framing is blunt about who this is for: "Search results are for people. Brave's LLM Context endpoint is for machines."</p>

<h2 id="goggles">Goggles: the feature no other API has</h2>

<p>Goggles let you define custom domain sets and re-rank results against them. You pick which sites are allowed to ground your model, and Brave reranks within that set. There are also extra snippets — up to five more per result — and schema-enriched results for structured data in any format.</p>

<p>This has no direct equivalent at Tavily, Exa, or Serper. If your agent answers questions about a specific documentation set, a regulatory corpus, or vetted vendor pages, Goggles turns "web search with a domain filter" into a controlled grounding source — the difference between a model citing whatever indexed and one citing only what you approved.</p>

<h2 id="answers-cost">Where the Answers tier gets expensive</h2>

<p>The Answers plan looks cheap at $4 per 1,000 requests. The token line is where the budget goes: $5 per million input and output tokens. Because a grounded answer consumes both the retrieved context and the generated response, one answer can bill meaningfully more than the request fee suggests.</p>

<p>Brave's own published benchmark frames the category, and deserves the usual skepticism about vendor-run benchmarks. On FreshQA, Brave reports 85.3% accuracy at $18 all-in per 1,000 questions, against Parallel Advanced at 79.0% for $49, Exa at 78.0% for $84, and Tavily at 78.0% for $89. Brave publishes the methodology, and is notably honest about the axis: all-in cost per 1,000 questions including search plus model tokens, not just the API price.</p>

<p>That is the right axis, and the one most vendor comparisons avoid: a cheap-per-request API returning long, noisy context can cost more per correct answer than a pricier one returning tight passages.</p>

<p>The honest caveat: 85.3% at $18 is Brave measuring Brave. The accuracy gap is real but far smaller than the price gap ($18 vs $49-$89), so if accuracy is your binding constraint the ranking may not move; if cost per correct answer is, the gap is large enough to matter.</p>

<h2 id="comparison">How Brave compares to the alternatives</h2>

<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>API</th><th>Pricing model</th><th>Index</th><th>Agent-shaped features</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Brave Search</strong></td><td>$5/1k requests; Answers $4/1k + $5/M tokens</td><td>Own independent index</td><td>LLM Context (1k-32k tokens, tables/code/JSON/captions), Goggles reranking, MCP server, x402 payments</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exa</td><td>Lite $4/1k, Search $7/1k, Auto $12/1k base; +$1/1k per result above 10; summaries $1/1k pages</td><td>Own index, semantic/neural retrieval</td><td>Answer endpoint, Agent endpoint, Monitors</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tavily</td><td>1 credit/call, $0.008/credit pay-as-you-go; free 1,000 credits/month</td><td>Aggregated web sources</td><td>Extract, Crawl, Map, Research, MCP server</td></tr>
<tr><td>Google CSE</td><td>100 queries/day free, then $5/1k up to 10k/day — <strong>discontinued Jan 1, 2027</strong></td><td>Google's own</td><td>None; closed to new customers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bing Search</td><td>—</td><td>Bing's own</td><td><strong>Retired Aug 11, 2025</strong></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Brave's FAQ frames the category as a vendor would: it can replace options with "smaller indexes (Tavily or Exa), higher latencies (SerpAPI or Serper), or more limited access (Google and Bing)," and complement localized options like Baidu, Naver, Yahoo, and Yandex. But the independent-index claim underneath it is a structural fact, and the two exits are dated facts now.</p>

<p>One practical consequence is that a search call rarely stays a search call. Once you run Brave for grounding, add a second engine for a coverage gap, and pay for both, the operational question stops being "which API" and becomes "who can see what I spent." A gateway such as <a href="https://portkey.ai/?ref=apirank" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Portkey</a> is a reasonable place to put that: one OpenAI-compatible interface across 200+ models, a "Gateway to Other APIs" feature that routes a search provider as a custom provider with full logging and monitoring, and virtual keys that give each provider its own spend cap and rate limit. Neither Portkey nor OpenRouter lists Brave in its own catalog today — this is about putting your Brave key behind a control plane, not about a bundled Brave plan.</p>

<h2 id="compliance">The two contract terms that decide your architecture</h2>

<p>Privacy marketing in this category is mostly noise, but two clauses in Brave's privacy notice and terms of service genuinely affect what you can build. Both are easy to miss and expensive to discover late.</p>

<p><strong>First: you may not train on the results.</strong> Clause (xiii) of the Brave Search API terms of service states that a customer shall not "use the Search Results to create, evaluate, train, re-train, fine-tune, benchmark or otherwise improve artificial intelligence models or services offered by Customer or third parties." If your plan is to build a dataset from Brave results, fine-tune on them, or even benchmark a model against them, the default terms forbid it. Storage is restricted the same way: clause (i) bars storing or caching Search Results other than "transient storage required for operation of Customer Applications."</p>

<p>That is the commercial logic of a vendor whose index is the asset: the API is licensed for grounding, not for data extraction. Brave's FAQ confirms the escape hatch is contractual — "Can I store results or use them to train models? Yes, with a custom plan."</p>

<p><strong>Second: "Zero Data Retention" is not the default, and it is scoped.</strong> Brave's privacy notice, updated November 3, 2026, states that a record of search queries submitted through a customer account "is retained for a maximum of 90 days for the purpose of billing Search API account holders and for troubleshooting." Zero Data Retention is an option, and the privacy notice's own data table marks it as available to Enterprise clients and "subject to Brave's legal obligations." The self-serve tiers get structural ZDR for the query path — Brave's framing is "every query ends on infrastructure we operate end to end, so there's no third party to retain it" — but not the 90-day billing log.</p>

<p>Brave calls this "architectural" rather than contractual ZDR, a real distinction, and the audit is genuine: <strong>SOC 2 Type II attested</strong>, independently verified and regularly audited. But if your requirement is "no query log exists anywhere, including ours," you need Enterprise, not the self-serve plan.</p>

<p>The privacy notice also makes explicit that all plans require payment information, which Brave says "is to help us safeguard against misuse of the service." For a card, it is never charged for free usage. The x402 path is the alternative for agents that pay per request without a card relationship — though the resulting transaction is recorded publicly and permanently on the Base blockchain.</p>

<h2 id="limits">Limits and things to know before you build</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Rate limits differ sharply by plan.</strong> Search allows 50 queries per second; Answers allows 2. If your agent fans out across many sub-queries, the Answers tier is the bottleneck, and no amount of prepaid credit changes it.</li>
<li><strong>Overages return HTTP 429, not a bill.</strong> Only successful requests count against your quota and are billed. Brave exposes <code>X-RateLimit-Limit</code>, <code>X-RateLimit-Policy</code>, <code>X-RateLimit-Remaining</code>, and <code>X-RateLimit-Reset</code> headers on every response, enforced over a 1-second sliding window, with monthly quotas expressed in seconds (<code>15000;w=2592000</code> is 15,000 requests per 30 days). Read <code>X-RateLimit-Reset</code> and back off on 429 instead of hard-coding a sleep.</li>
<li><strong>Prepaid credits, not subscriptions.</strong> There is no monthly plan on the self-serve tiers. You top up a balance. That is good for spiky agent workloads and bad for teams that want a predictable line item.</li>
<li><strong>Auth header is <code>X-Subscription-Token</code>.</strong> Not Bearer. Budget a shim if you are using a generic client. The Answers endpoint is the exception: it speaks the OpenAI chat-completions shape at <code>/res/v1/chat/completions</code> with <code>model="brave"</code>, so the OpenAI SDK works there with only a base URL change.</li>
<li><strong>Training and storing results need a custom plan.</strong> See the section above.</li>
<li><strong>CN latency is unmeasured.</strong> The API is US-hosted, with no China node. Expect the same cross-border latency as Tavily and route through a Cloudflare Worker or a Tencent Edge Function if it matters.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="verdict">Verdict</h2>

<p>Brave Search API is the strongest answer to a question that only recently became urgent: with Bing's search APIs retired in August 2025 and Google's Custom Search JSON API closing to new customers ahead of January 2027, the first-party search API market has narrowed to one vendor that still runs its own index. The reason is not price — at $5 per 1,000 requests it is exactly what Google charged, and Tavily and Exa are not far behind. It is that Brave alone sells an <strong>independent index</strong>, an <strong>agent-shaped context endpoint</strong>, and <strong>architected ZDR</strong> together, with no dated cliff of its own.</p>

<p>Pick it if you are grounding agents on a controlled set of vetted domains, if you want to price per correct answer rather than per request, or if you are replacing a Google CSE integration that has a January deadline. Skip it if you need a predictable monthly invoice, more than two answers per second, or if you intend to train on the results.</p>

<p>Two things to watch. The first is contractual, and covered above: the terms forbid training or benchmarking on the results, so if your roadmap needs extraction, that is a sales conversation, not a code change. The second is Brave's own claim that "all other services scrape." Treat that as a vendor claim — the independent index is verifiable, but the competitive generalization is not something to take on faith.</p>

<p>One practical note to close: Brave publishes no end-of-life date and no rate-change history for its own API. The one cliff you can verify today belongs to someone else — if you are moving off Google CSE, take the migration window you have rather than the one you assume.</p>

